WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.060
- [Johnny] It's January
2017 at the headquarters

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.910
of the African Union here in Ethiopia.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.710
An I.T. engineer is looking at
the servers in this building

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.383
and something seems off.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.710
The engineer notices that often
in the middle of the night,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:20.070
these servers are connecting
to some server in Shanghai

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.083
and uploading emails and audio files.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:30.350
The African Union Headquarters
was built in 2012.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.770
But it was not paid for
by any African nation.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.480
- [Reporter] The
impressive new headquarters

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.180
of the African Union

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.593
entirely funded by the Chinese government.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.050
- This building was a $200 million gift

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.370
from the Chinese government.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.150
It was designed and built

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.490
by Chinese firms with Chinese money.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:51.990
And yes, the servers were
installed by Chinese engineers

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.680
who, it appears, left
an intentional backdoor

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.603
into the servers so that
they were easily accessible.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.120
The African Union I.T. team eventually

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.570
removed these Chinese servers
and installed new ones.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.180
The Chinese government literally offered

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.890
to install them for
them, but they declined.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.570
The local I.T. people
also swept the building

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.240
and they found microphones

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.660
embedded in the furniture
and in the walls.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.800
The whole building was bugged
and it had been feeding audio

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.410
and internal emails to
China for like five years

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.570
since the building was built.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.120
China building a headquarters
for the African Union

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.370
and then spying on it
is a pretty good symbol

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.890
of what China is up to in Africa.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.530
And that's what this video is about.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.030
It's about how a growing
China has rushed into Africa

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.590
mainly by building a bunch of stuff,

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.690
roads, trains, dams, mines, ports.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:48.260
These projects are a part of
China's growth as a superpower.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.850
And it shows how this country
is projecting its power

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.670
on the continent of Africa.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:54.730
- [Reporter 2] There are questions here

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.090
over why the headquarters

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.780
of such an important African organization

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.960
is entirely the work

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.993
of the Chinese construction,
labor, and money.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.270
- Okay, so there's this
very predictable thing

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.570
that you see time and time
again as countries get powerful.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.310
As a country gets rich, they
often start looking outward

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.760
both economically and militarily.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.440
Let me explain what I mean here.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.300
50 years ago, China
was a very poor country

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.940
with a huge population.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.040
But eventually, they
opened up to the world.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.840
They opened up their huge
population as a workforce

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.680
for cheap labor and
their economy exploded.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.990
- [Reporter 3] China's
agrarian, urbanizing fast.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:33.780
You betcha. But still agrarian.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.733
- Most, in terms of land, not population.

00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:36.840
- Come on, Michael.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.563
- No, no. You're wrong about this.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.200
- So now, China is a very rich country.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.090
And remember, rich countries
start looking outward.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.650
How can they project their power,

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.530
connect with other countries
to continue their growth?

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.080
They do this for a couple of reasons.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.900
The first one is the economic reason.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.230
In the same way that the U.S.
got really rich and prosperous

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:00.120
and started to look outward
to China for cheap labor,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.320
China also got rich and it's
labor eventually got expensive,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.153
wages went up.

00:03:05.153 --> 00:03:08.780
And China needed its own
option for cheap labor

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:10.450
and new growth opportunities.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:13.500
So they started pouring
huge amounts of money,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.993
like $300 billion huge, into Africa.

00:03:16.993 --> 00:03:19.743
(rhythmic music)

00:03:25.347 --> 00:03:26.970
Okay, so that's the economic reason.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.900
China grows really fast.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:29.950
Their growth is slowing down.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.610
So they need to invest
in "emerging economies"

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:34.970
is what the economists would call it.

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:37.770
Economies that haven't
developed very quickly yet.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:39.480
But that with a little bit of investment,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:40.690
could start to grow.

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:41.940
Untapped markets.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.283
But the economic explanation
is only half the story here.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:50.060
In China, economics and government power

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.330
are not very separate.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.760
So to understand what
China is really up to,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.580
you have to understand
the geopolitical motives.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.680
And to do that, we got to look at the map.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.140
Here are all the countries
that China has projects in.

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:08.070
And yes, I just highlighted
every single country

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.660
on the African continent.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.870
I mean, besides this one down here,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.840
this little tiny country of Swaziland.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.890
They're the one holdout on all of this.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.530
These projects range from
transportation projects

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:20.380
like roads and trains

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:23.070
to energy like oil and dams

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.880
to real estate projects
like housing developments

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.060
and then mining for things like copper.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.580
You have everything from
a shoe factory in Ethiopia

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.980
to a $6 billion Chinese
finance to dam in Nigeria

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:39.230
to a massive suspension
bridge in Mozambique,

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:40.813
the longest in Africa.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.150
I mean, these projects are everywhere.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.380
Even here on this tiny
island off the coast

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.781
where China has poured
millions of dollars to build

00:04:50.781 --> 00:04:54.440
housing, a stadium, an
auditorium, a library,

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.060
as well as their national
parliament, try to build it all.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.740
There are too many of these
projects to even count.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.150
And honestly, if you take it as a whole,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:06.340
a lot of these projects
have been a major benefit

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:09.480
and economic stimulus to
these countries' economies.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.410
But why is China doing this?

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:12.570
What are they up to?

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.600
Well, in addition to
all the economic value

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.760
of investing in emerging markets,

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.950
China's president also claims
that they're doing this

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.118
in the name of cooperation and friendship.

00:05:23.118 --> 00:05:27.127
(Xi Jinping speaking in Chinese)

00:05:27.127 --> 00:05:28.980
"The African way."

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.640
Let's dig into this and actually see

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.270
what these projects look
like and how they are built.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:37.113
And if it truly is "the African
way", whatever that means.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.980
Okay, so there's this really
impressive rail system

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.040
that China built in Kenya.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.640
It goes from Nairobi to Mombasa
in just under four hours,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.580
which is faster than any
train in the United States.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.100
Which, let's be honest here,
isn't really saying much

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.270
because here in the United States,

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:54.900
we don't really know how
to build trains anymore.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.500
We used to be really good
at it and now we're not.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.660
It's kind of a shame and... Oh.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.615
Anyway, look at this train in Kenya.

00:06:00.615 --> 00:06:03.910
(rhythmic music)

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:08.910
This railway system was
a $3.6 billion project,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.280
the most expensive infrastructure project

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.070
since Kenya's independence.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:14.790
And it was mostly entirely paid for

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:18.440
by a giant loan from
China's state run bank.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:21.530
So it was financed by China,
designed by Chinese engineers,

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:23.900
and then built by Chinese
construction firms

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.480
that sent Chinese workers to hire locals

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.970
and manage the project.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:29.460
And now, it's a Chinese company

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:30.820
that actually runs the thing.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.210
If you ride this train,

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.440
you're gonna see all of
the safety announcements

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:38.220
and all of the documentation
in Chinese, in Mandarin.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.340
It is very clear who financed
and built this thing.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.280
It looks like China just parachuted in

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:45.050
and plopped this thing down in Kenya.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:48.510
And now, Kenya has
billions of dollars of debt

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:50.430
that they have to pay
back to China eventually.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:55.430
In fact, 72% of all of
Kenya's debt is owed to China.

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:58.300
And this railway was
meant to reduce congestion

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.410
on the highway that runs parallel to it,

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:01.490
but it hasn't done that.

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:03.782
In fact, this railway cuts right through

00:07:03.782 --> 00:07:06.390
like these two national parks.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.090
- [Reporter 2] While the
train will offer passengers

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.150
a view of a lifetime while passing through

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:13.150
the national park,
conservationists are concerned

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.640
that this construction
will affect the ecosystem

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.540
and the wildlife that live here.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.020
- And it's actually not doing

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.090
what it said it was going to do.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.440
So yes, Kenya has this big shiny railway

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.830
but it wasn't really solving

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:27.030
African issues in the African way.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:29.070
Meanwhile, the Chinese
construction workers

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:30.170
who came to build this thing

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.360
were caught treating the
local workers very poorly,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.530
making them sit at separate
tables during meal breaks,

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:39.010
punishing them when they
didn't complete deadlines

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.600
or take out the garbage on time.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.990
That mixed with the fact that
some of these Chinese workers

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:46.810
were taking bribes as a part
of a huge corruption scheme

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.400
with this railway.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.410
And it's clear that this project

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:54.680
wasn't actually about supporting
the local environment.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:57.600
It just left Kenya with
a giant pile of debt

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.250
and a big shiny railway.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.050
But listen, that's not always the case.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:02.480
There are tons of these projects

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:05.730
and some of them are
truly helping the locals

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.880
like this big, beautiful,
shiny road here in Lesotho.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.580
This used to be a rough gravel road

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.230
and it is now a beautiful, shiny road

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:16.830
that is helping the locals
move around in their country.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.750
So it's definitely a mixed bag.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:19.890
And listen, I get it.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.000
China, you want to give a $5 billion loan

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.740
to Nigeria to build a dam

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.530
because Nigeria has 200 million
people, many of them young.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:31.300
And you want to help them
become a booming economy

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.500
so that you can get in
on some of that growth.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.500
Oh, and you love the fact
that you don't have to adhere

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.280
to any environmental standards

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:38.480
while you're doing these projects,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:39.960
which I know you love, China.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:40.793
I know it.

00:08:40.793 --> 00:08:42.070
That's the business rationale.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:44.500
We're looking for new
economies to invest in

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:45.890
and be a part of their growth.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.460
But if you look a level deeper,

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.330
you won't just see a bunch
of investment projects.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:53.190
You'll see a bunch of
free gifts from China.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:56.160
And I'm intrigued by
especially the political ones.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.210
Like when China shows up and builds

00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.440
like a new parliament building

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.470
which they've done for
seven countries in Africa.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:06.800
Or when China offers
32 million free dollars

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:08.810
to build the new headquarters for ECOWAS

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:11.990
which is basically the economic
union in Western Africa.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:13.120
They do this for free.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:14.672
We will build you a new headquarters.

00:09:14.672 --> 00:09:16.680
(sighs) Well, let me be clear.

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.630
There is no such thing as a free gift

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:21.100
in international relations.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:22.743
Everything has strings attached.

00:09:22.743 --> 00:09:26.290
So a major part of all of
this investment in Africa

00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:30.153
is China investing in its
future as a global superpower

00:09:30.153 --> 00:09:32.430
who needs to have friends

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:34.730
and has, historically,
not had a lot of friends

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:35.670
throughout the world

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.220
because the big boy United
States has been doing

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.470
all the friending by giving people stuff

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:43.390
and making their lives better
so that they can be allies.

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:45.220
So China is getting in on this.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:48.010
And China has used their
checkbook to make friends

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:51.900
with literally every
single country in Africa.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.090
Of course, besides Swaziland,

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:55.840
the teeny little country in the south

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:57.260
that has been the one country

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.750
that has held out on all of this.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:01.570
In funneling all of this
money into these countries,

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:04.580
China has been able to get
all of these countries,

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.690
besides Swaziland, to not
recognize Taiwan anymore

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:11.020
and to support China instead.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:14.370
The TLDR on that is that you
can't support Taiwan and China

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:15.700
'cause they both think that they're China.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.290
So you have to support one or the other.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.270
And so, China has been getting
everyone to denounce Taiwan

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:21.480
and say that they support China.

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.175
That's a whole other thing.
I'm making videos about that.

00:10:23.175 --> 00:10:26.550
Suffice it to say that China
has been using its checkbook

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:28.700
to get all of these people
to be their friends.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:31.570
It's sort of like the
rich kid in middle school

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:34.590
who, like, brings you an MP3 player

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.310
because he wants you to be his friend.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:39.870
And you're like, "Sweet. If
you give me your MP3 player,

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:40.703
I'll be your friend."

00:10:40.703 --> 00:10:42.650
I grew up in the early 2000's.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.030
In my middle school,
there were MP3 players.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:47.760
So, sorry for the throwback.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.060
But remember when China built
the African Union Headquarters

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:53.110
and then bugged the entire building

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:55.840
so that they could spy on the leaders

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.840
and their emails and their conversations?

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.800
That is a perfect representation

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:03.070
of the strings that are usually
attached to these projects.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:05.440
But perhaps the less obvious

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.118
but more dangerous strings
attached are the debts.

00:11:10.118 --> 00:11:12.701
(upbeat music)

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:16.087
John Adams said it best
when he said, quote,

00:11:16.087 --> 00:11:19.350
"There are two ways to
conquer and enslave a country.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:22.620
One is by the sword and
the other is by debt."

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:25.150
It's a pretty intense quote,
but it really applies here.

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:27.020
So many of these projects

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:30.530
have been paid for by
Chinese money through loans.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.910
African nations diving deep into debt.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.760
We all know that when
you owe someone money,

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.020
that person has a lot of power over you.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:38.290
Especially if you get to the point

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.010
where you can't pay them back.

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:41.700
And this isn't just theoretical.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:46.070
A few years ago, China
helped Sri Lanka build a port

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:47.800
here in the Southeastern coast.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:49.360
It was done with a Chinese loan.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:52.600
With time, Sri Lanka struggled
to pay back that loan.

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:55.907
So in 2017, China swooped in and said,

